{"archetypes":[{"name":"caller-contract","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"Plan claims _stage_and_push_ci_fixes is a 'no-op-then-push' but the function also calls run_checks_with_lint_fix_loop (relevant-checks) when checks_site is provided, which the plan never mentions; both callers supply checks_site.","prompt_body":"The plan asserts that after lint-fix-loop emits applied with a coder-committed fix, run_per_job_local_fix_loop returns 0 (ok) and _stage_and_push_ci_fixes at scripts/ship-pr.sh:1585 acts as a no-op-then-push. Read that function: at line 1600-1603 it calls run_checks_with_lint_fix_loop when checks_site is provided, and both callers (lines 1746 and 1973) always supply checks_site. Verify whether that embedded relevant-checks run could itself dispatch another coder, which might commit again and re-enter the head-changed scenario the fix is trying to resolve. Also check the baseline_clean=false edge case (plan Edge Case #3): when the baseline is dirty and the coder commits, capture_tracked_dirty_paths at line 1597 still picks up those lingering dirty files, and the git add at line 1621 may stage and double-commit them alongside the already-committed coder fix — reconcile this with the plan's 'the commit IS the fix' statement. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"stub-schema","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"Plan adds LINT_FIX_HEAD_CHANGED=true to the emit protocol but the test-ship-pr.sh stub (lines 261-268) has no STUB_LINT_FIX_HEAD_CHANGED variable, leaving the preferred rewrite path unimplementable as written.","prompt_body":"The plan introduces emit_kv LINT_FIX_HEAD_CHANGED true and proposes rewriting the ship-pr head-changed test to stub STUB_LINT_FIX_STATUS=applied STUB_LINT_FIX_COMMIT_SHA=<sha> STUB_LINT_FIX_HEAD_CHANGED=true. Read the stub shim at scripts/test-ship-pr.sh lines 257-268: it echoes LINT_FIX_STATUS, FAILURE_REASON, and LINT_FIX_DELTA_PATHS_FILE from STUB_* env vars, but has no branch for LINT_FIX_HEAD_CHANGED. Without extending the stub, the preferred rewrite path cannot emit the new field and the plan's assertion that the test can verify LINT_FIX_HEAD_CHANGED=true is unfounded. Verify whether the plan's 'Acceptable fallback: delete the case' option is also fully safe — specifically, check whether any other ship-pr test asserts on the head-changed stall path that would become a coverage hole if the case is removed. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"pipefail-guard","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The proposed forbidden-path pipeline assigns git diff into a variable under set -euo pipefail with no error guard; an invalid ref at that point aborts the entire script rather than falling back to the detached-HEAD failure path.","prompt_body":"The plan's pseudocode computes committed_forbidden_count via a pipeline: git diff --name-only baseline_head..current_head | awk ... | wc -l | tr -d ... The enclosing script uses set -euo pipefail (per .claude/rules/shell-strict-mode.md), and with pipefail active a non-zero exit from git diff (e.g., one ref unresolvable mid-dispatch) propagates through the $(...) assignment and aborts the script entirely — bypassing the planned fail_status 'forbidden-path-violation' 1 path and leaving the caller without a clean failure reason. The plan says 'guard git diff failures explicitly if needed' but the pseudocode shows no guard. Verify what the current script does when git rev-parse fails during similar ref lookups (e.g., line 286 uses || fail_status) and confirm the plan should specify an analogous explicit guard here. Also check whether an empty forbidden_paths_file (no submodules) causes the awk NR==FNR pattern to produce a well-formed committed_forbidden_count=0 rather than an awk error or empty variable. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
